          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-Lof"}> Per Martin-Löf
            ‘s
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic
              type theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#MartinLof75"}> Martin-Löf 75
            ), or
            %strong constructive type theory
            is a specific form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            developed to support
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            that contain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            or are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic
          %p Martin-Löf’s dependent type theory is notable for several reasons:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                One can construct an interpretation of first-order
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
                by interpreting
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
                (this is true of most any dependent type theory).
            %li
              %p
                It has a version of a variant of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                %em as a theorem
                (because of the properties of the above interpretation), see the discussion
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#AxiomOfChoice"} below
            %li
              %p
                In its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional
                form, it has sufficient computational content to function as a programming language. At the same time, it then has
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
                whose presence shows that one is really dealing with a form of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h2#formal_presentation Formal presentation
          %p
            There are many different ways to present Martin-Löf dependent type theory in a formal system. In this article, we give a formal presentation of Martin-Löf dependent type theory in the framework of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            and no
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
          %h3#JudgmentsAndContexts Judgments and contexts
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of Martin-Löf dependent type theory can be constructed in two stages:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The first is the
                %em raw
                or
                %em untyped
                syntax of the theory consisting of expressions that are readable but not necessarily meaningful.
            %li
              %p
                The second stage consists of defining the
                %em derivable judgements
                of the type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} inductively
                which then pick out the meaningful
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
            of succesively
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            may be defined as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Bruijn+indices"} de Bruijn indices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is given by
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
          %p
            One may also define contexts as coming with a variable name, in which case one needs a notion of
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p Types and terms are built inductively from various constructors. Types, terms and contexts are defined mutually.
          %p
            We have the basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            forms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}
              %p
                saying that: “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
                is a well-formed context”;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash A\; \mathrm{type}
              %p
                saying that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is a well-typed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed "\u201D;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash a : A
              %p
                saying that “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                is a well-typed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed "\u201D;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
            The following table indicates the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics+of+dependent+types"} of these notions of dependent type theory
          %center
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DependentTermsSyntaxAndSemantics-230326.jpg", :width => "650"}/
          %p
            We work in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} logical framework
            of
            = succeed "," do
