          %p …
          %h3#intensional_and_extensional_type_theories Intensional and extensional type theories
          %p
            In Martin-Löf dependent type theory, one makes a distinction between intensional and extensional type theories. A Martin-Löf dependent type theory is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
            if there is an equality reflection rule where from typal equality of terms, one could derive definitional equality of terms:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi A
                      %mi b
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ≡
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma, a:A, b:A \vdash p:a =_A b}{\Gamma, a:A, b:A \vdash a \equiv b:A}
          %p
            The type theory is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            if it doesn’t have the equality reflection rule.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#models_and_categorical_semantics Models and categorical semantics
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            of ML type theory depend crucially on whether one considers the variant of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+type+theory"} extensional type theory
            or that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
          %p
            The models of the extensional version are (just)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+categories"} locally cartesian closed categories
          %p
            The faithful models of the intensional version with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            are however certain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+categories"} simplicial model categories
            (
            %a{:href => "#Warren"}> Warren
            ). For this reason one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            For a more detailed discussion of these matters see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %h3#AxiomOfChoice Axiom of choice
          %p
            In dependent type theory we can verify the following “logical form of the
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bell"} Bell
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Tait"} Tait
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Rijke"} Rijke, section 2.5.1
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (ACL)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi acl
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} apply
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x : A, y : B(x) \vdash C(x, y) : Type
                    \\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;
                    acl
                    \:
                    (\forall x : A) (\exists y : B(x)) C(x, y) \to (\exists f : (\Pi x : A)B(x) )(\forall x : A) C(x, \mathrm{apply}(f, x))
                    \\,.
          %p
            One should note carefully that this “is” only “the axiom of choice” under the above propositions-as-types interpretation of the quantifiers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
              of this expression, using the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions-as-types"} propositions-as-types
              logic corresponds roughly to working with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+lattices"} subobject lattices
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ex%2Flex+completion"} ex/lex completion
              of a
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              in its ex/lex completion.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              If we use instead a different logic over the same type theory, such as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bracket+types"} bracket types
              to model the actual subobject lattices in an arbitrary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} lccc
              (not necessarily the ex/lex completion of anything), or the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hProp"} hProp
              logic in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
